{
  "id": "01-number-of-islands",
  "title": "Number of Islands",
  "track": "Graphs",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": [
    "graphs"
  ],
  "source": "canonical Number of Islands concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The input is a grid of cells, where each cell holds 1 for land or 0 for water. Land cells connect horizontally or vertically, never diagonally. An island is a connected group of land cells. Return how many islands the grid contains.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= number of rows <= 100",
    "1 <= number of columns <= 100",
    "grid values are only 0 or 1"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    "grid"
  ],
  "functionName": "num_islands",
  "starterCode": "def num_islands(grid):\n    # grid[i][j] is 1 for land, 0 for water; return the island count.\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": {
        "grid": [
          [
            1,
            0,
            1
          ],
          [
            0,
            1,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            0,
            1
          ]
        ]
      },
      "output": 5,
      "explanation": "The four corners are separate islands, and the center is a fifth."
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "grid": [
          [
            1,
            1,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            0,
            0
          ],
          [
            0,
            0,
            1
          ]
        ]
      },
      "output": 2,
      "explanation": "The three land cells in the top left connect into one island."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Walk every cell; each unvisited land cell you meet belongs to exactly one island you have not counted yet.",
    "When you find unvisited land, visit its whole connected group right away and mark every cell of it so it never counts twice.",
    "Flood fill: from the found cell, reach up, down, left, and right recursively or with a queue, marking land as you pass."
  ],
  "solution": "Scan every cell; on each unvisited land cell, run a flood fill (stack or queue) that marks the entire connected group as visited, and count one island. Every cell is entered once: O(rows * cols) time and space.",
  "solutionCode": "def num_islands(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(rows):\n        for j in range(cols):\n            if grid[i][j] != 1:\n                continue\n            count += 1\n            stack = [(i, j)]\n            grid[i][j] = 0\n            while stack:\n                x, y = stack.pop()\n                for nx, ny in ((x + 1, y), (x - 1, y), (x, y + 1), (x, y - 1)):\n                    if 0 <= nx < rows and 0 <= ny < cols and grid[nx][ny] == 1:\n                        grid[nx][ny] = 0\n                        stack.append((nx, ny))\n    return count\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def num_islands(grid):\n    def island_cells(start):\n        cells = [start]\n        queue = [start]\n        visited = {start}\n        while queue:\n            x, y = queue.pop()\n            for nx, ny in ((x + 1, y), (x - 1, y), (x, y + 1), (x, y - 1)):\n                if 0 <= nx < len(grid) and 0 <= ny < len(grid[0]) and grid[nx][ny] == 1 and (nx, ny) not in visited:\n                    visited.add((nx, ny))\n                    cells.append((nx, ny))\n                    queue.append((nx, ny))\n        return cells\n    seen_minima = set()\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(len(grid)):\n        for j in range(len(grid[0])):\n            if grid[i][j] != 1:\n                continue\n            cells = island_cells((i, j))\n            low = min(cells)\n            if low not in seen_minima:\n                seen_minima.add(low)\n                count += 1\n    return count\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include all water, all land, and single-cell grids",
  "expectedComplexity": {
    "time": "O(m * n)",
    "space": "O(m * n)"
  },
